  "problem": {
    "id": "train-3370",
    "question": "A watermelon stand sold 46 watermelons. Seventeen customers bought one melon, three customers bought three melons, and the rest bought two melons. How many customers bought two watermelons?",
    "gold": "10",
    "answer": "The customers that bought one watermelon bought 1 * 17 = <<1*17=17>>17 melons.\nThe customers that bought three melons bought 3 * 3 = <<3*3=9>>9 melons.\nThe stand sold 46 - 17 - 9 = <<46-17-9=20>>20 melons to customers who bought 2 melons.\nThus, 20 / 2 = <<20/2=10>>10 customers bought two watermelons.\n#### 10"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine how many customers bought two watermelons, we start by calculating the total number of watermelons sold to the customers who bought one or three watermelons.\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of watermelons sold to the customers who bought one watermelon each:\n\\[ 17 \\text{ customers} \\times 1 \\text{ melon/customer} = 17 \\text{ melons} \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of watermelons sold to the customers who bought three watermelons each:\n\\[ 3 \\text{ customers} \\times 3 \\text{ melons/customer} = 9 \\text{ melons} \\]\n\nAdding these together gives the total number of watermelons sold to the customers who bought one or three watermelons:\n\\[ 17 \\text{ melons} + 9 \\text{ melons} = 26 \\text{ melons} \\]\n\nSince the total number of watermelons sold is 46, the number of watermelons sold to the customers who bought two watermelons is:\n\\[ 46 \\text{ melons} - 26 \\text{ melons} = 20 \\text{ melons} \\]\n\nSince each of these customers bought two watermelons, the number of customers who bought two watermelons is:\n\\[ \\frac{20 \\text{ melons}}{2 \\text{ melons/customer}} = 10 \\text{ customers} \\]\n\nThus, the number of customers who bought two watermelons is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).",
